WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.520
Here's the deal, guys: the market for
these handheld gaming PCs is booming, and

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.560
the Steam Deck is the main
driver of that growth.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.160
Not that these PCs didn't exist
before, but when everyone saw the Steam

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.100
Deck, they went: "I... I want it.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.000
I don't know why, but I want it."

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.100
Then Valve and lots of companies went
that way, making devices like this, and

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.600
the most successful one was ASUS.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.920
This device here was made by
them. It's the ROG Ally X, or ROG

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:30.140
Ally X, however you want to say it.
Don't correct me in the comments, please.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.320
People will do it anyway.
It's like a Steam Deck Pro.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.200
Its processor is a Ryzen Z1 Extreme,
more powerful than the Steam

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.400
Deck's. It has more RAM and
a screen that isn't OLED like

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.839
the Steam Deck OLED's, but it's
a Full HD LCD at 120Hz with

00:00:44.879 --> 00:00:47.040
variable refresh rate support.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.760
On top of all that, the ROG Ally
has an 80 watt-hour battery,

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.400
versus 50 on the Steam Deck OLED
and 40 on the Steam Deck LCD.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.840
That's laptop battery size. Way bigger.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.760
The engineering it took to fit
this thing in here is impressive.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.060
It's practically perfect,
except for one thing.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.060
It has Windows.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:07.520
It comes...

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.200
with Windows! Let me explain for
anyone not up to speed on this.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.160
The Steam Deck runs an operating system
called SteamOS, which is based on

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.320
Linux, but it runs Windows games.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.300
How does it run Windows games
if Linux is a different system?

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.600
It uses a compatibility layer called
Proton, which translates the instructions

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.320
of a Windows game into Linux.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.040
Do all compatible games run perfectly?

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.640
No, but most do.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.400
So, wait a minute, if not every
game runs properly on it, isn't it

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.260
better to use Windows?

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.360
Because on Windows, every Windows
game runs without a problem.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.680
It's better to have all the games
than almost all the games, right?

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.099
Right?

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.840
No, wrong. That's what we thought
when the Steam Deck came out, but

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.720
then we used Windows 11 on these
devices with tiny little screens, only a

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:03.299
touchscreen, no keyboard and mouse,
and the experience is infuriating.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.880
So much so that I have several devices
more powerful than the Steam Deck, but I

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.360
always end up going back to the Steam
Deck because I don't want to deal with

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.320
Windows. "Oh, but not every
game works on the Steam Deck,

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.640
you're gonna be..." Who cares,
who cares. Let me explain.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.920
Starting with the classic Windows problems
that show up when you boot the device

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.440
for the first time.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.360
It'll try to push a Microsoft Office
install on you with every major update

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.040
to Windows, to try to sell
you the subscription later.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:37.480
Why? Because Microsoft Office is
Microsoft's second biggest revenue source.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.200
If you get distracted and click
the wrong button once, it installs.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:44.460
Is Microsoft Office a bad app suite?

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.640
No, it's actually very good.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.880
Do I need it on a small handheld
made for playing video games?

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.240
Absolutely not. And the incompetence is
worse because Microsoft could be pushing

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.960
Game Pass with the same effort they're
putting behind Microsoft Office,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.080
which is also a Microsoft subscription
service, for games, and would make way

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.040
more sense for a device like this.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.400
But Microsoft is in no hurry, right?

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.440
Because the company's other
divisions make billions.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.480
They're swimming in money, let's be real.

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:16.360
But so far I've only talked about first
boot and operating system updates,

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.680
because updating a handheld like
this is a problem all on its own.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.760
Let's say you're playing a Steam
game on it. You already have

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:27.160
to keep your game updated
through the Steam app.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.240
Count with me, that's number 1.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.180
But you also need to open Windows
Update in the settings to keep

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.960
Windows updated, and that's number 2.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.520
Then you have to open Armoury Crate
to update some drivers and the

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:43.160
gaming interface ASUS
runs on top of Windows, to

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.720
make the user experience less painful.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.600
Thanks, ASUS, but that's already 3.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.520
You also have to update stuff through
the MyASUS app, which is standard

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.280
on every ASUS PC, to download
drivers like Bluetooth and audio.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:57.720
And that makes 4.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.260
One of these 2 ASUS apps
can also update the AMD

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.440
GPU, but you'll want AMD's
own app too, Adrenalin, to

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.680
control some of the GPU's features.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.640
So that's one more place to update
yet another thing on your device.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:12.680
That's 5.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.540
5! Meanwhile, how does it
work on the Steam Deck?

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.440
You have your game update, all
normal, and one update in the

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.880
settings menu for everything
else, and all of it in a single

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:32.840
app. Poor ASUS puts a lot of effort
into improving the Windows experience,

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.600
which is why it comes with Armoury
Crate and Command Center to manage

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:40.640
lots of aspects of the device, but
nothing can fix Windows' problems.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.600
That's Microsoft's job.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:47.480
Like, on the Steam Deck, the extra
features to control power draw, frame rate,

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.680
are all mapped to the controller.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:51.200
Not on Windows.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.960
On the Steam Deck, you can suspend
a game and come back later knowing

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.599
it'll still be working.
Not on Windows.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.640
And there's a lot more,
we could be here all day

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.560
talking about it, but you get the idea.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.880
Not just you, Microsoft too.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:09.600
So much so that Microsoft's VP of
next generation recently said they're

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:13.160
working on a version of Windows made
specifically for these handhelds.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.579
They're working on it, which is good.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.180
Is it available?

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:22.320
No. So what we can do is
install SteamOS on the

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.799
ROG Ally, and then we get
the best of both worlds.

00:05:25.839 --> 00:05:32.499
Battery, a 120Hz screen with variable
refresh rate, a more powerful processor and

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:34.019
no Windows.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.099
No Windows.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.120
But here's the catch.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.040
Valve, who makes the Steam Deck,
promised to make SteamOS available

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.760
for everyone to download and
install wherever they wanted.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:47.699
They promised. Did they deliver?

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:52.480
Not yet. However, there's a huge
however here, because SteamOS is

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:55.440
Linux. Linux is free and open source.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:59.600
If Valve won't give you
SteamOS, you can go and make it

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.600
yourself. Anyone can.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:07.000
You just need a lot of programming
knowledge, a lot of Linux knowledge and

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.760
a lot of free time.
But luckily for us, someone did.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.239
More specifically, a group
of very dedicated people.

00:06:14.279 --> 00:06:18.480
They maintain a project called
Bazzite, a Linux distribution in which

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:23.799
they went and replicated everything
SteamOS does, down to the last detail.

00:06:23.839 --> 00:06:28.160
Except where they couldn't, since different
devices have different hardware, but

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:29.920
like, it's basically the same thing.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.120
So you can install
SteamOS wherever you want.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.960
They make it available for
you to download for free.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.960
Totally for free, you can turn
your device into a Steam Deck.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:46.160
So, ladies and gentlemen, now we're
going to install Bazzite, i.e. SteamOS

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.920
on the ROG Ally X and see the results.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:53.120
Let's go! First, I had to download
Bazzite from their website.

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:57.440
I just clicked download, picked the
ROG Ally X as my device and the KDE

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:00.160
desktop environment so it matches SteamOS.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:03.440
Then I downloaded an app, Balena Etcher,
to make an installation flash drive

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.600
with the Bazzite ISO
I had just downloaded.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.600
In Balena Etcher, I selected the ISO
and the flash drive I'd write it to,

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.440
and that was it, I had a Bazzite
installation flash drive.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:16.800
Then on the ROG Ally X, I went into the
security settings and decrypted its SSD.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:21.520
Then I downloaded a program called
Paragon Partition Manager to split my SSD

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:25.520
in two, and I gave 512 gigabytes
to the partition where I'd install

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.680
Bazzite. Then I plugged a USB hub
with a keyboard and mouse, and my

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:32.960
Bazzite flash drive, into the Ally
X and restarted it holding volume

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.440
down to get into the BIOS.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.640
In the BIOS, I went to boot priority
and dragged the flash drive up

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:38.520
so it's first.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.200
Then I clicked the top left
button and clicked save.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.720
When it booted the device from the
Bazzite flash drive, I told it to install.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.880
Then I selected the language,
then the install location.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.440
Since the SSD was already partitioned,
I just clicked Done, and then I

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:54.720
clicked to set a username and password
and to go ahead with the install.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.480
After installing, it restarts
into the secure boot menu.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.639
I clicked Enroll MOK, Continue,
Yes, and typed in the password

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:04.840
Universal Blue, which is
from the Bazzite folks.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:06.600
Then it rebooted and that was it.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:10.939
It opened straight to the menu to
enter my Steam login. It's installed.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:14.240
But seriously, guys, when I started
working on this video, I thought: this'll

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.600
be a fun experiment, a
cool thing to test, right?

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.520
See how it works.
But let me tell you something.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.720
This is amazing. It's amazing!

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:27.920
The ROG Ally with Bazzite is basically a
Steam Deck Pro, everything we asked for.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:31.600
It works wonderfully, and you get
the 120Hz screen with variable

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:34.920
refresh rate, you get the bigger battery,
and you also get the processor that's

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:36.000
way more powerful.

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:39.560
It has every Steam Deck feature.
I can click here and open

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.040
that menu to control performance,
I can control the screen's refresh

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:48.440
rate, I can turn on the
screen's variable frame rate.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.400
You can control the screen
scaling and even that performance

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:57.060
overlay. I'll turn it on here
and set it to the biggest one

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:58.800
here, done. The most detailed one.

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:01.900
Here are the numbers so I can track
the game's performance the same way

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.000
I can on the Steam Deck.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:06.440
The only thing actually missing
is the power control, but what

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:09.880
you can do here is click
on it twice and it'll

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:11.320
give you this control here.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:14.720
You can change how much
power the device draws.

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:19.139
And if your game freezes, you can
use this menu here to close it too.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.080
Confirm.
Done. And yes, everything works.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:29.200
You have access to your library here, it's
all there, just like on the Steam Deck.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:33.200
And if you need to update your
system or change anything on it, you

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:38.539
can go to settings, come here
to System and check for updates.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.360
There aren't any, we're good.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:46.240
And just like you do on the
Steam Deck, you can use an

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:48.960
external controller to
bring up these menus.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:51.120
In fact, the button mapping is the same.

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:53.000
And you can play tons of games on it.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.040
I tested a bunch, even games without
official support, that aren't,

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:59.099
quote unquote, Great on Deck.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:03.000
And on top of that, you can play
lots of older, lighter games at

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:06.600
120Hz, because that's its
screen's max refresh rate.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:10.519
Obviously, some games aren't
supported, aren't Great on Deck, and

00:10:10.559 --> 00:10:14.240
will give you trouble, but they give
you trouble on the Steam Deck too.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:18.160
But the best part, ladies and gentlemen:
for games that don't work on Steam

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.420
Deck/Bazzite, you can do this, look:

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:22.760
Power,

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.339
restart,

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:30.460
confirm, and hold the
up button, volume up,

00:10:31.559 --> 00:10:32.680
while it restarts.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:37.040
Then you get to its dual
boot screen, and you can pick

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:42.040
Windows, because I kept Windows on the
machine. I have both operating systems.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:45.600
Here we have it, ladies and
gentlemen, Windows 11 running.

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:49.800
All the features are still here. You
can use this for unsupported games,

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:52.380
but also for Game Pass games.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.400
ASUS sure loves its little noises, huh?

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:03.360
But I'll confess something:
I left 512 gigs for Bazzite

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.720
to use, and the rest
of the SSD for Windows.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:09.400
If I had to do this all over again,
I'd leave much less space for

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:14.160
Windows, because I don't see myself
going back to Windows for anything.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:17.880
At most, I think I'd leave room
for just one game that might

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.240
not work on Bazzite. But at least
there's one thing that saves the day,

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:25.280
and that's its SD card.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:29.519
Steam games you install on it
are available to Windows and also

00:11:29.559 --> 00:11:33.680
to Bazzite. So with one install,
you can open the game on both.

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:36.720
You can even use it to compare
performance if you want.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:41.280
So now back to Bazzite, because
I have 2 last things to show you.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:45.200
First, SteamOS screen recording works.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:49.080
If I come here to Media, I've
already recorded some files. You can

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:53.800
record internally, straight from the
overlay menu on top of the game, and

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:57.320
you've got your gameplay
recorded at up to 1080p.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:02.160
And finally, the amazing thing that's
a total game changer for this kind

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:04.219
of device, which is this:

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:06.339
You put it in standby,

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:08.620
it goes into standby.

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:10.200
You come back

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:12.339
No cuts.

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.380
It's right back where you were.

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:17.219
How hard can it be, Windows?

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:22.600
Why can't a billion-dollar company like
Microsoft, or trillion, I don't know,

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:27.120
it's probably past a trillion,
not sure, make this work reliably?

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:28.519
Seriously, check this out.

00:12:28.559 --> 00:12:31.600
This one is Tembo, if I'm not
mistaken. It's not great on

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:33.960
Deck, no, quote unquote, official support.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:36.799
I'm playing the game, I go and pause,

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:38.859
click to suspend,

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:43.320
it suspends, then I come back later,
like this, when I want to play

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:44.539
and click again.

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:49.280
It's exactly where I left off!

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:52.040
I can unpause and keep playing.

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:53.440
Like magic, right?

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:57.400
But okay, guys, now for the
downside. In all my time with Bazzite

00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:01.160
on the ROG Ally X, I had
2 fairly serious problems.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:05.880
First, it took more work than I'd
like to pair Bluetooth controllers

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:09.840
with it. I don't know what's going
on, it lists a ton of devices,

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:13.800
I select the controller, it
doesn't always connect, but once I

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:17.200
managed to pair the controller,
it worked without any problems.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:20.000
And one thing I couldn't
do is use the shortcut to

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:21.560
bring up the on-screen keyboard.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:25.480
You do it by pressing the Steam
button and the X button together, but

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:30.600
it works on an external controller,
not when you're using it on the

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:34.280
ROG Ally. From what I read online,
there's a hardware limitation on this

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:38.760
ROG Ally button being used together
with another button on the device.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:40.519
So you can't trigger both at once.

00:13:40.559 --> 00:13:43.280
And I know it's a ROG
Ally limitation and not a

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:45.960
Bazzite one, because it works
with an external controller.

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:49.480
Apparently there's a way to fix it.
Someone already made a hack you can

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:51.240
download online to get it working.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:56.200
But the point of this video was to
see how easy it is to install Bazzite

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:00.960
on the ROG Ally X and use it
without having to configure much.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:02.820
That plug and play experience.

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:04.800
So I ended up not doing it.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:08.720
If you're looking to do something
like this, keep this limitation

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:11.400
in mind. Some games ask for a keyboard.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:14.400
Why? No idea, but you have to
open a keyboard to get through the

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:18.240
menu. And the only game
rated Great on Deck that

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:20.880
gave me trouble was Spider-Man 2.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:24.560
It froze completely on me while I
was playing. I had to restart the

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:28.480
device, but I think that's a
problem with the game and not the

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:32.080
system itself, because it has a reputation
for being really poorly optimized.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:35.600
Sony is putting out some patches,
but this game's PC launch definitely

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.320
wasn't smooth.

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:41.440
But out of everything I've seen so
far, this was the best experience

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:45.000
I've had with one of
these handheld gaming PCs.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:48.120
Seriously, guys, I don't think I
can overstate this. I see no reason

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:52.680
to go back to the Steam Deck or
use any other experience that isn't

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:55.000
this one or something very close to it.

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:59.220
And Valve knows it, so much so that
recent leaks say they're already

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:03.680
preparing the generic version of
SteamOS for you to install on your

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:07.440
device. You won't even need
Bazzite, but Bazzite itself

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:12.240
is already extremely impressive, because
this is a community effort, guys.

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:17.320
It's people volunteering their time to
create this kind of system you can install

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:18.440
on your device.

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:21.860
And here you don't get the problems
you get using Windows 11, which

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:25.280
is an operating system from
a billion-dollar company.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:26.240
I don't get it.

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:29.920
And a device like the ROG Ally
or ROG Ally X is great for

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:34.880
this kind of install, because it's
so popular that you'll most likely

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:36.840
find support for everything on it.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:41.280
The Bazzite folks even make a specific
build for you to install on it and

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:44.000
have it work right away,
with nothing to add on top.

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:47.920
An advantage you wouldn't have if
you tried installing Bazzite on more

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:50.880
niche devices, like the AYANEO,
which I've reviewed on the channel.

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:54.600
To get it working on a device like that,
you'd probably have a lot more work and

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:56.920
you'd have to install a
lot more stuff on top.

00:15:56.960 --> 00:16:00.700
But anyway, if you have a device like
this and you're thinking of trying

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:04.520
Bazzite, I'll put links to some
guides in the description, and don't

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:07.000
forget to leave a like and
subscribe to the channel.

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:09.640
I think I'll see you next time.
Bye.
